WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.540
One blood sample gives you one number.

2
00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:07.180
What can it tell you?

3
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:10.540
A hormone level is one point on a moving curve.

4
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.600
Hormones are released in pulses, then cleared.

5
00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:18.360
Growth hormone left plasma with an average half-life of 27 minutes.

6
00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:23.720
That was one small study's mean, after a large intravenous dose in five people.

7
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:28.700
A sample drawn just before a pulse can read many times lower than one drawn on its

8
00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:29.700
peak.

9
00:00:29.783 --> 00:00:31.523
So read two hormones together.

10
00:00:32.083 --> 00:00:33.243
Take the cortisol axis.

11
00:00:34.023 --> 00:00:38.743
CRH, the hypothalamic signal, drives ACTH from the pituitary.

12
00:00:39.383 --> 00:00:42.583
ACTH drives the adrenal cortex to make cortisol.

13
00:00:43.183 --> 00:00:47.183
Call ACTH the trophic hormone and cortisol the target hormone.

14
00:00:47.783 --> 00:00:51.803
Cortisol then feeds back on CRH and ACTH.

15
00:00:52.043 --> 00:00:55.403
That is long -loop feedback, with strong evidence at both levels.

16
00:00:56.317 --> 00:00:58.357
Now break the chain at the gland.

17
00:00:59.277 --> 00:01:04.057
In this made -up example, adults on a normal sleep -wake schedule have blood drawn

18
00:01:04.057 --> 00:01:05.057
at 8 in the morning.

19
00:01:05.837 --> 00:01:12.757
Person A has cortisol of 3 micrograms per decilitre and ACTH of 300 picograms per

20
00:01:12.757 --> 00:01:18.237
millilitre. Low cortisol should make an intact pituitary drive hard, and it does.

21
00:01:18.937 --> 00:01:22.257
The adrenal does not answer: that is the primary pattern.

22
00:01:23.197 --> 00:01:25.557
Reference intervals differ by laboratory and assay.

23
00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:32.640
Person B has the same low cortisol, but ACTH is only five picograms per millilitre.

24
00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:34.560
The drive itself is missing.

25
00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:40.380
That points to the pituitary, a secondary disorder, or to the hypothalamus, a

26
00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:45.860
tertiary one. The pair cannot say which, because either lesion lowers ACTH.

27
00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:48.720
It can separate gland disease from central disease.

28
00:01:49.583 --> 00:01:52.203
Now change one input and watch the output.

29
00:01:53.023 --> 00:01:55.743
Person B receives synthetic A -C -T -H.

30
00:01:56.303 --> 00:02:00.363
Thirty minutes later, cortisol is 16 micrograms per decilitre.

31
00:02:01.103 --> 00:02:05.723
Whether that is adequate depends on the assay, because criteria for newer, more

32
00:02:05.723 --> 00:02:09.123
specific assays sit below the historical one.

33
00:02:09.683 --> 00:02:14.663
An adequate response shows only that the adrenal can answer A -C -T -H.

34
00:02:15.203 --> 00:02:20.683
After a recent loss of central drive, the adrenal cortex may not yet have atrophied.

35
00:02:21.023 --> 00:02:26.383
So a normal result does not exclude a recent pituitary or hypothalamic defect.

36
00:02:27.283 --> 00:02:29.123
The thyroid reads the same way.

37
00:02:29.683 --> 00:02:34.503
High TSH with low free T4 is the classical primary pattern.

38
00:02:35.043 --> 00:02:41.083
Low free T4 with low or inappropriately normal TSH points to central disease.

39
00:02:41.663 --> 00:02:46.823
A hormone level is a point on a moving curve, and a criterion belongs to its assay.

40
00:02:46.823 --> 00:02:50.963
Read hormones as pairs, and know which assay made the number.
